{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1787082867,"nonce":"0kVBbADuoEBYMB6L3Sp1LITtIL4=","target":"752cb1856bfaf358c06601bcc8f04a200bb180b4d53ad593d7f72fec918fa417","message":"**What is wrong.** The flap detector was retired on ONE premise: that the case retention window W blinds `flap_score`, because a flap absorbed inside W appends neither `case.opened` nor `case.resolved` and so falls below `flap_threshold` exactly when the alert is flapping hardest. That premise is true, and it is conditional on `W \u003e 0`. **W is `NOT NULL DEFAULT 0` over a table that starts empty, and a missing row is 0.** On every deployment that has not written a `case_policy_config` row for a given `(namespace, alertname)` — which is every deployment on day one and every pair nobody has configured on every other day — nothing is absorbed, both events append normally, and the detector was correct. It was deleted anyway, and the justification never states the condition it depends on.\n\n**What is wrong.** The flap detector was retired on ONE premise: that the case retention window W blinds `flap_score`, because a flap absorbed inside W appends neither `case.opened` nor `case.resolved` and so falls below `flap_threshold` exactly when the alert is flapping hardest. That premise is true, and it is conditional on `W \u003e 0`. **W is `NOT NULL DEFAULT 0` over a table that starts empty, and a missing row is 0.** On every deployment that has not written a `case_policy_config` row for a given `(namespace, alertname)` — which is every deployment on day one and every pair nobody has configured on every other day — nothing is absorbed, both events append normally, and the detector was correct. It was deleted anyway, and the justification never states the condition it depends on.\n\n**The demonstration.** The premise is stated three times and qualified nowhere. `db/migrations/00060_no_enum_remembers_a_damper.sql:35-40`: *\"the case retention window W damps a flap AT CASE FORMATION, so the events `stateChangeCountsSQL` counts fall BELOW `flap_threshold` exactly when an alert is flapping hardest. A detector that lies is worse than no detector.\"* SPEC §B.6.2 says the same in longer form. `internal/alerts/repository/alert.go:1226-1245`, the `SetFlap` tombstone, says it a third time: *\"Six flaps that used to append twelve counted events append about two, against a threshold of five over two hours.\"*\n\nNow the other half of the same repository. `db/migrations/00057_a_case_outlives_the_flap.sql:124` declares `retention_window_s INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0`, and `:29` says in the header that *\"`retention_window_s` is `NOT NULL DEFAULT 0`, the table starts EMPTY\"*. `CasePolicyRepository.RetentionWindow` (`internal/alerts/repository/casepolicy.go:105-121`) returns `0, nil` on `pgx.ErrNoRows` and collapses a stored `0` to the same answer, under a comment that spells out the consequence: *\"NO ROW MEANS ZERO, AND ZERO MEANS TODAY'S BEHAVIOUR... 0 makes the §B.3 T5 arm take no deferral branch at all, which is the pre-00057 close path unedited.\"* ADR 0041 Amendment 1 proves the rest, in a section titled *\"W=0 changes nothing, and the T5 arm's ORDER is the whole argument\"*: all three added branches are false at W=0, so *\"at W=0 the executed statement sequence **is** the pre-00057 arm.\"* An unedited T5 appends `case.resolved`; the next fire opens a new Case and appends `case.opened`. Six flaps append twelve events, against a threshold of five. The detector sees exactly what it always saw.\n\n`stateChangeCountsSQL` and `EventRepository.StateChangeCounts` are still here and still correct (`internal/alerts/repository/event.go:521-600`); `internal/alerts/service/deps.go:84` records that they *\"DELIBERATELY SURVIVE with no consumer\"*. What was deleted around them is the wiring: `AlertRepository.SetFlap` — the only statement that ever wrote either column (`alerts/repository/alert.go:1213`, tombstone) — `SetFlap` on the service port (`alerts/service/ports.go:60-61`), `Service.ScoreFlaps` and `FlapResult` (`alerts/service/sweep.go:714`), the `EventCounter` port (`alerts/service/deps.go:77`), the `flap.score` job kind (`internal/platform/jobs/kinds.go:54-63`), its args (`internal/platform/jobs/args.go:502-506`) and its handler and periodic tick (`internal/app/workers.go:311-317`).\n\n⚠️ **And W is no longer SQL-only, which makes the blindness narrower still and also makes ADR 0041's own statement of it stale.** The amendment says *\"W is settable only by SQL today. A grep for `case_policy|retention_window_s|CaseRetention` over `api/`, `web/`, `deploy/` and `docs/` returns nothing.\"* That grep now returns four routes. `internal/alerts/api/router.go:196` mounts `/case-policies`; `internal/alerts/api/casepolicies.go:33,64,119,153` serve list, create, patch and delete; `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:1830-1930,6894-6930` carries `CasePolicyDTO` and `createCasePolicy`; and `internal/app/container.go:549,568` wires `casePolicyConfigRepo`, so the routes answer rather than 503. W is therefore configurable — opt-in, per `(namespace, alertname)`, and off until somebody writes a row.\n\n**Why it matters.** git-bug `1816a42` is the ticket W exists to resolve, and its done-when 6 is *\"`notifications.suppressed_reason` no longer admits `flapping`, and the flapping digest is deleted — retention makes it unnecessary. **`is_flapping` remains visible.**\"* The first two clauses landed; the third was reversed. And the ticket's stated payoff was named in its **Why it matters**: *\"one case across the flap, one notification, one thread reply, and an operator who can still see `is_flapping` and go fix the Prometheus rule that is missing a `for:`.\"* That operator no longer exists. The column keeps whatever value the retired job last wrote (`alerts_flap_ck` still stands, `alertColumnList` still selects it, `?flapping=` still filters on it at `alerts/repository/alert.go:778` and the rollup still counts it at `:996`) — but nothing recomputes it, so on a fresh database every row reads `false` forever. `web/src/features/alerts/filters.ts:122-130` removed the web facet for exactly that reason, and is right to: a facet over a blind column returns a confidently empty list.\n\nSo the alert-hygiene affordance is gone, and it was gone for a reason that does not apply to the default configuration. SCOPE-BOUNDARY §3.4's advice — *\"this rule has `for: 0s`, which is why it flaps\"* — has no data behind it any more.\n\n**This is a decision to revisit, not a bug to reverse.** The repo owner chose retirement. The framing that decision was made on did not mention that W's default is 0, that the table starts empty, or that the blindness is therefore scoped to pairs an operator has explicitly opted in. Whichever way it goes, the reasoning on the page should be the corrected one, because the current text asserts unconditionally something that is conditionally true.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/00057_a_case_outlives_the_flap.sql:29,124,140,154` (W's default and the empty table) · `internal/alerts/repository/casepolicy.go:105-121` (`RetentionWindow`, no-row-means-zero) · `docs/adr/0041-the-alert-case-allocation-and-the-rule-that-decides-it.md:227-250` (the restatement, \"W=0 is the degenerate case and it is the shipped default\"), `:411-470` (*the flap detector went BLIND*, the ruling), `:495-510` (the stale \"settable only by SQL\" note and the refusal to teach the score to see) · `docs/design/SPEC.md` §B.6.2 · `db/migrations/00060_no_enum_remembers_a_damper.sql:29-40` · `internal/alerts/repository/alert.go:1213-1250` (the `SetFlap` tombstone, where the premise is stated a third time), `:778`, `:996` (the reads that survive) · `internal/alerts/service/ports.go:60-61` · `internal/alerts/service/sweep.go:714` · `internal/alerts/service/deps.go:77-90` · `internal/platform/jobs/kinds.go:54-63`, `internal/platform/jobs/args.go:502-506` · `internal/app/workers.go:311-317` · `internal/alerts/repository/event.go:521-600` (the input query, intact and unconsumed) · `web/src/features/alerts/filters.ts:122-130` · `internal/alerts/api/router.go:196`, `internal/alerts/api/casepolicies.go` (W's settings surface, which the ADR says does not exist) · git-bug `1816a42`, done-when 6.\n\n**Scope.** medium — the decision is the work, and each of the three outcomes is a small change.\n\n**Done when.** One of the three is chosen and the page says which, with the W=0 default named in the reasoning rather than absent from it:\n\n1. **Restore the detector and scope its blindness.** `SetFlap`, the port method, `ScoreFlaps`, the `EventCounter` port and the `flap.score` job come back — `stateChangeCountsSQL` never left, so the input is already there — and the score is computed only for Alerts whose `(namespace, alertname)` has `retention_window_s = 0`. Where W is configured the columns are left untouched rather than written false, because W is that pair's flap answer and a stale-but-honest reading beats a fresh lie. The web facet returns.\n2. **Teach the score to see.** The held resolve is fed back through a new `alert_events.type` for an edge that records a resolve without performing it. ADR 0041 refuses this and SPEC §B.6.2 records the refusal; reopening it means an API-contract change and a codegen run, and the refusal's reasoning (\"one damper is the point\") is unchanged by the W=0 default.\n3. **Confirm the deletion with corrected reasoning.** §B.6.2, `00060`'s header, ADR 0041's amendment and the `SetFlap` tombstone all stop asserting the blindness unconditionally and instead say: the detector is blind where W is configured, is correct where it is not, and is retired anyway because a signal that is right on the default and wrong on the opt-in is not one an operator can act on. Whichever text lands, it also has to say what replaces `1816a42`'s payoff — an operator finding a rule with no `for:` — or record that nothing does.\n\nIn every case ADR 0041's *\"W is settable only by SQL today\"* paragraph is corrected: `/api/v1/case-policies` exists, is wired, and has four operations.","files":null}]}